  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nTroughing progressing over the Great Lakes and southeast Canada will \ncontinue to slide east tonight and Thursday. The associated surface \ncyclone centered near James Bay will gradually weaken with time as \nthe system becomes increasingly vertically stacked and favorable \nadvections for support are choked off. Meanwhile, ridging will \nquickly amplify across the middle of the country late this week and \nhold through the holiday weekend, building surface high pressure \nacross Hudson Bay/Ontario that will attempt to nose down into the \nGreat Lakes by Saturday/Sunday. Ridging may finally begin to fold \novertop of the region heading into the middle of next week as \ntroughing works across the west from the Pacific coast.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nThe ongoing line of showers with a few embedded storms will keep \ntracking east across northern lower Michigan this afternoon. Given \nthat outflow from this morning's round of storms pushed well south \nof the CWA into lower Michigan and the airmass will only be able to \npartially recover ahead of this, strong storms appear unlikely at \nthis time. Relatively speaking, the best chance for any strong storm \nwill come across our far southeast CWA near Saginaw Bay. Most likely \nscenario is that showers and a few storms track through and \nstrongest activity is confined south of the area, primarily in the \nthumb of lower Michigan this afternoon. After this line passes, dry \nweather is expected for most areas with some clearing cloud cover \nthrough the remainder of the day.\n\nThe brief break in rain chances may come to an end later tonight. \nAdditional showers and a few storms may form along the warm front \nacross mid Michigan, perhaps working into the M-55 corridor into \nearly Thursday morning. Mainly dry conditions are expected for most \nof the area through the morning hours until another round of showers \nand storms could move into northern lower by the afternoon. Best \nchances for showers/storms will come along and south of M-72. While \nsome instability and shear aloft could support a few strong storms, \ncurrent confidence is that convective mode will limit this potential \nand severe storms are not anticipated at this time.\n\nLooking ahead, rain chances diminish as a whole as time goes on\nFriday and this weekend. Subsidence will build surface high \npressure increasingly into the region, keeping dry weather is \nplace for the most part -- especially late this weekend. \nSeasonable temperatures are also anticipated with highs in the \n70s and low 80s and overnight lows in the 50s for most areas.",
